      "urpx:forRatePlan": {
        "@id": "gpc:tou-eol"
      },
      "urpx:hasCoverageClaim": {
        "@id": "gpc:cc-tou-eol-2026-06-01",
        "@type": "urpx:CoverageClaim",
        "urpx:completenessResult": {
          "@id": "urpx:partial"
        },
        "urpx:selfContained": true,
        "urpx:explainedBy": {
          "@type": "urpx:Evaluation",
          "@id": "gpc:cc-tou-eol-2026-06-01-evaluation",
          "urpx:evaluationKind": {
            "@id": "urpx:coverageEvaluation"
          },
          "urpx:description": "This filing does not carry everything its source document states. 6 parts are deferred, listed here in the same words as the filing's tariff.toml [modeling].deferrals so the two records cannot drift. (1) Fuel seasons by billing month. FCR-27 prices June through September and October through May, which Schedule R's bills show is read by the billing month a bill is rendered in. The JSON-LD gates the fuel PriceDefinitions on calendar months, so usage in the weeks a billing period runs ahead of its billing month takes the other season's fuel price. (2) Voltage level and franchise fee location. FCR-27's three voltage levels and MFF-11's two locations are crossed in one ProfileAlternatives set of six alternatives, because URPX v0.5.1 allows one set per PlanElements and the franchise fee's energy subtotal must include that voltage level's fuel prices. A consumer selects one alternative, for example Secondary Distribution inside city limits. (3) Municipal Franchise Fee base. MFF-11 applies to the total revenues of each bill, which include the ECCR-15 and DSM-C-15 amounts. Its multiplier Prices here are based on the energy-and-fuel subtotal and on the non-energy charges' Prices, not on the ECCR and DSM-C multiplier Prices, so the franchise fee on those two amounts, about 0.44 percent of the base bill inside city limits, is not carried. (4) Demand Side Management schedule. The sheet applies \"the Company's effective Demand Side Management Residential or Commercial Schedule\". DSM-C-15 applies to all Commercial Customers as the Company's Rules and Regulations for Electric Service define them, and is carried here as Prices with no condition, so it applies to every customer on the schedule. A Residential Customer served on the schedule would pay DSM-R-16, 1.1969 percent, instead; the sheet names no schedule for an Industrial Customer. Neither case is carried. (5) Fuel Cost Recovery schedule. The sheet applies \"the Company's effective Fuel Cost Recovery Schedules in the manner ordered by the Georgia Public Service Commission\" without naming one, and neither TOU-FCR-7 nor TOU-FCR-TP-5 lists TOU-EOL. The JSON-LD carries FCR-27's seasonal rates; if the Commission ordered a time of use fuel rate for the pilot, a modeled bill's fuel is off by the difference. (6) Pilot limits and Term of Contract. The pilot is limited to 100 accounts selected at the Company's discretion; the term is one year, renewed automatically unless terminated with 30 days' notice before the anniversary, and the customer may change tariffs after the first twelve months on 30 days' written notice. The limits are stated in the eligibility rule's description; nothing is priced by them and they are not carried.",
          "urpx:evaluatedOn": "2026-10-19T00:00:00"
        }
      }
    }
  }
